bilities of all three implementations; it cannot run before they land. |\n| *(deliberate non-edge)* server impl ⇸ operator impl | Rule 13 / Rule 14 | The operator's rendered Deployment references the server's flag combination — a configuration reference, not a build-against artifact (different repos, no shared artifact). The coupling is expressed as the operator epic's \"artifacts-only Deployment runs with the combined flags\" AC, never as a DAG edge; the two epics run in parallel. |\n\n## Conditional Branches\n\n**E004 (routing design) has two bounded outcomes:**\n\n- **Outcome A — ingress-level routing suffices** (artifact traffic routed to the dedicated\n  instance at the Gateway API / HTTPRoute level; no server change): the main plan covers it\n  entirely within the operator epic; no branch file.\n- **Outcome B — the main tracking server needs a configuration change to delegate artifact\n  operations**: additional server-side work not owned by any main-plan epic, planned as\n  `RHAISTRAT-8009-BRANCH-B-E009.md` (branch=B, gated_by E004, `remove` on outcome A).\n\n## HLR Traceability Matrix\n\nHLRs numbered in strategy order.\n\n| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| HLR-1 `--artifacts-only` combinable with `--enable-workspaces` + `--workspace-store-uri` | P0 | E006 | End-to-end verified in E008. |\n| HLR-2 Artifact requests validated against workspace membership; non-members denied 403 | P0 | E006 (enabled by E005) | Resolution capability from the plugin epic; end-to-end verified in E008; bypass resistance informed by E002. |\n| HLR-3 Fail-closed when workspace store unreachable | P0 | E006 | Failure-mode matrix from E003; end-to-end verified in E008. |\n| HLR-4 Single-tenant / no-workspace-flag deployments retain current behavior | P0 | E006 | Regression verified in E008. |\n| HLR-5 Operator deploys dedicated artifacts-only Deployment + Service + HTTPRoute | P1 | E007 | Verified in E008. |\n| HLR-6 Main tracking deployment routes artifact traffic to the dedicated instance | P1 | E007 (+ BRANCH-B-E009 if routing outcome B) | Routing mechanism determined by E004. |\n| HLR-7 Auth plugin workspace resolution works in artifacts-only context | P1 | E005 | Approach determined by E001. |\n| HLR-8 Audit logging for workspace access denials through server logs | P2 | E006 **(bundled)** | Bundled, not split: the audit log statements are emitted at the same denial sites HLR-2 implements, in the same request-handling code — a separate epic would modify the same files twice, so the P2 work is not independently schedulable. |\n\nFull coverage: every HLR maps to at least one epic; no orphaned requirements.\n\n## Health Warnings\n\n1. **Component vocabulary collapse** — \"MLflow Operator\" and \"mlflow-kubernetes-plugins\" have\n   no canonical Jira component entries; all epics carry canonical component `MLflow` (see\n   Component Mappings). Jira component-level triage will not distinguish operator/plugin work.\n2. **mlflow-kubernetes-plugins has no dedicated architecture-context file** — it is documented\n   indirectly by mlflow.md as the external `kubernetes-auth` plugin package (v1.5.0, installed\n   from PyPI). Per the strategy's dependency table (\"internal, exists\") it is treated as an\n   existing in-platform component: **no onboarding chain generated**. Flag for verification: if\n   the plugin's source repo is not accessible for midstream changes, an onboarding chain\n   (repo/license/konflux) would need to be added.\n3. **Strategy-level process gates pending human review** — Architecture Review, ODH/RHOAI Build\n   Onboarding, Licence Validation, Accelerator/Package Support, Documentation Support, UXD\n   Support, Performance Team Support are all \"Pending human review\". These are strategy-level\n   gates, not epic-scoped work; no epics were generated for them. The pending architecture\n   review should be resolved before implementation epics merge.\n4. **Audit telemetry channel (OQ, PM/Security)** — HLR-8 commits to server logs. If\n   PM/Security selects OTLP or the Kubernetes audit log instead, the server epic's audit scope\n   extends additively; no structural change to this decomposition.\n5. **Latency target undefined (OQ, PM/Engineering)** — no latency target exists in the RFE or\n   architecture context. The server epic carries a measure-then-optimize AC (short-TTL caching\n   if the agreed target is exceeded). If caching is introduced, its staleness behavior must\n   satisfy the security analysis (E002).\n6. **`--artifacts-only` mode is not corroborated by architecture context** — mlflow.md\n   corroborates `--enable-workspaces`, `--workspace-store-uri kubernetes://`,\n   `--app-name kubernetes-auth`, and `--serve-artifacts` (Dockerfile.konflux CMD) but does not\n   describe the `--artifacts-only` mode. Not contradicted; noted for signal-9 honesty and as a\n   verification item inside E001.\n7. **Epic size — E006 (server)**: ~1-2 sprints by the strategy's effort arithmetic (above the\n   ~2-week guideline). Kept whole because flag composition, workspace-provider initialization,\n   and per-request validation are one server initialization/request-handling change over the\n   same code path — a split would build the same request surface twice. Re-split trigger: if\n   the error-handling/failure-mode work emerging from the fail-mode analysis exceeds ~half a\n   sprint beyond the strategy's estimate, split the failure-mode hardening into a follow-up\n   epic rather than growing this one past 2 sprints.\n8. **Epic size — E007 (operator)**: ~2-3 sprints by the strategy's effort arithmetic. Kept\n   whole because the dedicated Deployment/Service/HTTPRoute templates and the artifact traffic\n   routing configuration are one declarative topology over the same Helm-in-operator render\n   path — splitting would fragment one chart surface across epics that build the same\n   templates. Re-split trigger: if the routing design (once resolved) shows the traffic-routing\n   co