{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-eef1eec7-4efb-4f01-8e84-38b91b81b246","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-96518007df3f8645","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8009-9kjmo_fl/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8009-E002.md","content":"# Security analysis: bypass vectors for workspace-aware artifact serving\n\n## Description\n\nAdversarial security investigation for a tenant-isolation enforcement change. This strategy\nmakes artifact access control a security boundary in multi-tenant deployments (workspace\nmembership validation with a fail-closed mandate), so a dedicated bypass-vector analysis is\nrequired before the main implementation. The investigation enumerates the ways an artifact\nrequest could evade workspace validation in the planned split-server topology and produces\nthe mitigation requirements that the server, plugin, and operator implementations must\nsatisfy.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Threat analysis of the artifact request path in workspace-aware artifacts-only mode:\n  artifact paths that do not map to an experiment, malformed or manipulated paths (including\n  encoding and traversal variants), and paths that could resolve to the wrong workspace.\n- Analysis of topology-level bypass vectors in the split-server deployment: artifact requests\n  reaching an artifacts-only instance that lacks workspace flags (misconfiguration), traffic\n  routing that sends artifact requests to an instance without workspace validation, and any\n  residual artifact-serving surface on the main tracking deployment that skips validation.\n- Derivation of concrete mitigation requirements per surface: server-side request validation,\n  plugin-side resolution hardening, and operator-side configuration validation (the strategy's\n  risk mitigation: operator validation should reject configurations where the artifacts-only\n  instance lacks workspace flags).\n- If short-TTL caching of workspace membership decisions is introduced for latency, analysis\n  of cache staleness/invalidation as a bypass vector (stale membership decisions must not\n  grant access after a workspace assignment changes).\n- Deliverable: a bypass-vector analysis document mapping each vector to a determination, the\n  required mitigation, and the implementation scope (server / plugin / operator) that must\n  carry it.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- The analysis enumerates the request-path and topology-level bypass vectors listed above\n  and, for each, records a determination (possible under the planned design / mitigated by\n  design) with supporting evidence.\n- Every vector determined possible has a corresponding mitigation requirement assigned to a\n  specific implementation scope (server request handling, plugin resolution, or operator\n  configuration validation).\n- The operator-side validation requirements (rejecting misconfigured split-server\n  deployments where the artifacts-only instance would lack workspace flags) are stated\n  precisely enough to serve as acceptance criteria for the operator implementation.\n- Any caching-related staleness vector has a defined invalidation/consistency requirement\n  consistent with the fail-closed mandate.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\nInvestigation epic — no HLR ownership. De-risks HLR-2 and HLR-3 (tenant isolation and\nfail-closed denial) and informs the configuration-validation aspect of HLR-5 and HLR-6.\n\n## Risks / Notes\n\n- Workspace authorization reuses the existing auth plugin infrastructure; per Staff Engineer\n  input, this analysis must not prescribe Kubernetes API mechanics (SelfSubjectAccessReview\n  details are an mlflow-kubernetes-plugins implementation concern, not strategy-level).\n- The kube-rbac-proxy sidecar and `--app-name kubernetes-auth` authentication layer are\n  unchanged by this strategy and out of analysis scope except where they interact with the\n  new validation path.\n"}}],