{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-6da27b8f-af7e-4bec-8563-46e58746e8d0","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-aa2686076f96b067","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8009-9kjmo_fl/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8009-E004.md","content":"# Determine artifact traffic routing design for the split-server topology\n\n## Description\n\nInvestigation resolving the strategy's open question: how the operator should configure\nartifact traffic routing from the main tracking deployment to the dedicated artifacts-only\ninstance. The answer affects the HTTPRoute design and determines whether the main tracking\nserver needs a configuration change to delegate artifact operations — which in turn\ndetermines whether additional server-side work exists beyond the main plan.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Evaluate the candidate routing approaches at a functional level:\n  - Ingress-level routing: Gateway API HTTPRoute configuration routes artifact traffic to\n    the dedicated artifacts-only instance, with no change to the main tracking server.\n  - Server-side delegation: the main tracking server is configured to delegate artifact\n    operations to the dedicated instance.\n  - A combination of both, if the analysis shows either alone is insufficient.\n- For each candidate, assess feasibility against the operator's existing patterns — the\n  Helm-in-operator rendering with server-side apply, and conditional Gateway API HTTPRoute\n  creation based on runtime CRD discovery — and the operational surface of the split\n  topology (two Deployments, two Services, two HTTPRoutes).\n- Assess the impact on the main tracking deployment for each candidate, including\n  backwards compatibility when split-server artifact serving is not enabled.\n- Recommend one approach with rationale, identify exactly what changes in the operator, and\n  state explicitly whether any main-tracking-server configuration change is required.\n- Deliverable: a routing design decision document with bounded outcomes — (A) ingress-level\n  routing suffices with no server change, or (B) server-side delegation configuration is\n  required.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- The decision document evaluates the candidate approaches and recommends one, with\n  feasibility evidence grounded in the operator's existing rendering and routing patterns.\n- The document states explicitly which bounded outcome holds — (A) ingress-level routing\n  suffices, or (B) the main tracking server requires a configuration change to delegate\n  artifact operations — since this selects the conditional branch of the decomposition.\n- The HTTPRoute design implications for the dedicated artifacts-only instance are described\n  at a functional level sufficient to implement the operator's routing configuration.\n- Backwards compatibility is addressed: deployments that do not enable split-server\n  artifact serving are unaffected by the chosen design.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\nInvestigation epic — no HLR ownership. De-risks HLR-6 and determines whether additional\nmain-tracking-server delegation work exists (conditional branch of the decomposition).\n\n## Risks / Notes\n\n- Owner per the strategy's open questions table: MLflow Team.\n- The strategy's risk table flags split-deployment operational complexity: misconfiguration\n  could let artifact requests bypass workspace validation. The chosen design must compose\n  with the operator validation that rejects configurations where the artifacts-only instance\n  lacks workspace flags.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":93329,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a71db037e41f9110","agent_id":"a42b59692a3812c5b","session_id":"36784e31-2123-4326-9e59-f3bb80327bfe","uuid":"3795d0fa-6683-407d-91eb-b0e6abaed39a","timestamp":"2026-10-26T19:41:57.301Z","suba