{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-c6c2531f-13d1-4870-9f8f-3570813c0b29","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-b032b30ba9689e16","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8009-9kjmo_fl/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8009-E006.md","content":"# Workspace-aware artifacts-only serving in the MLflow tracking server\n\n## Description\n\nMakes `--artifacts-only` composable with `--enable-workspaces` in the MLflow tracking\nserver. When both flags are set, the server requires an explicit `--workspace-store-uri`,\ninitializes only the workspace provider needed to validate artifact requests, and validates\nevery artifact request against workspace membership before serving. Tracking stores and\nmodel registry stores stay uninitialized, and non-artifact API endpoints remain disabled.\nNo new server flag is introduced — the workspace-aware behavior emerges from combining the\nexisting flags (Staff Engineer input). The design is fail-closed: if workspace validation\ncannot be completed, artifact requests are denied rather than served without validation.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Flag composition: allow `--artifacts-only` to combine with `--enable-workspaces`; when\n  combined, require an explicit `--workspace-store-uri` and fail to start with a clear error\n  if it is absent.\n- Initialize only the workspace provider needed to validate artifact requests; tracking\n  stores and model registry stores remain uninitialized; non-artifact API endpoints remain\n  disabled.\n- Workspace validation of artifact requests: resolve the artifact path to its parent\n  experiment (artifacts are stored under experiment-scoped paths), look up the experiment's\n  workspace assignment via the workspace store, and delegate authorization to the existing\n  auth plugin infrastructure. Requests failing workspace validation are denied with HTTP 403.\n- Fail-closed error handling implementing the failure-mode to response matrix: every\n  failure mode (workspace store unreachable, resolution errors, plugin exceptions, malformed\n  or partial store responses, timeouts) denies the request — including HTTP 503 with an\n  indication that workspace validation is unavailable when the workspace store is\n  unreachable. No fail-open path.\n- Backwards compatibility: `--artifacts-only` without `--enable-workspaces` retains current\n  behavior; deployments that do not set `--enable-workspaces` are unaffected; no existing\n  API contract, CLI argument, or configuration key changes meaning.\n- Audit logging of workspace access denials through server logs, with sufficient context to\n  audit (requesting identity, artifact path, workspace, outcome).\n- Performance: measure the per-request latency overhead of workspace validation; if it\n  exceeds the target agreed with PM/Engineering, implement short-TTL caching of workspace\n  membership decisions consistent with caching patterns already used in the tracking\n  server's auth path, honoring the security analysis's cache-staleness requirements.\n- Document limitations for non-standard artifact roots (initial implementation scoped to\n  the S3 artifact store, per the strategy's risk mitigation).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- Given the artifacts-only instance started with `--artifacts-only --enable-workspaces\n  --workspace-store-uri kubernetes://`, a user who is a member of workspace A requesting an\n  artifact belonging to an experiment in workspace A is served successfully (HTTP 200 with\n  correct artifact content).\n- A user who is a member of workspace A but not workspace B requesting an artifact belonging\n  to an experiment in workspace B is denied with HTTP 403 and an error message indicating\n  insufficient workspace permissions.\n- Given `--artifacts-only` set without `--enable-workspaces`, any authenticated user is\n  served without workspace validation (current behavior preserved).\n- Given the workspace store unreachable, artifact requests are denied with HTTP 503\n  indicating workspace validation is unavailable (fail-closed).\n- Startup with workspace-aware artifacts-only mode and no explicit `--workspace-store-uri`\n  fails with a clear error.\n- Every failure mode in the failure-mode to response matrix denies the request; no code\n  path serves an artifact when workspace validation is active but incomplete.\n- Workspace access denials are recorded in server logs with enough context to audit.\n- Per-request latency overhead of workspace validation is measured and reported against the\n  target agreed with PM/Engineering; where the target is exceeded, short-TTL caching of\n  workspace membership decisions is implemented.\n- Rollback / feature flag: workspace-aware artifact serving activates only when both\n  `--artifacts-only` and `--enable-workspaces` are set; removing either flag restores the\n  prior artifacts-only behavior with no residual enforcement path.\n- Single-tenant deployments and configurations without workspace flags show no behavior\n  change (regression).\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- HLR-1 (P0): `--artifacts-only` combinable with `--enable-workspaces` and\n  `--workspace-store-uri` for workspace-validated artifact serving.\n- HLR-2 (P0): artifact requests validated against workspace membership; non-members denied\n  with HTTP 403.\n- HLR-3 (P0): fail-closed behavior when the workspace store is unreachable.\n- HLR-4 (P0): single-tenant and non-workspace deployments retain current behavior.\n- HLR-8 (P2) — bundled into this epic: audit logging of workspace access denials through\n  server logs is emitted at the same denial sites HLR-2 implements, in the same\n  request-handling code; a separate epic would modify the same files twice, so the P2 work\n  is not independently schedulable.\n\n## Risks / Notes\n\n- The error-handling acceptance criteria adopt the failure-mode to response matrix from the\n  fail-mode analysis; the path-resolution and hardening requirements adopt the security\n  bypass analysis findings.\n- No latency target is specified in the RFE or architecture context (open question owned by\n  PM/Engineering); the measure-then-optimize acceptance criterion above is the mitigation.\n- Workspace resolution capability in the artifacts-only context is delivered by the\n  `mlflow-kubernetes-plugins` workspace resolution work; this epic builds against it.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":94919,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a71db037e41f9110","agent_id":"a42b59692a3812c5b","session_id":"36784e31-2123-4326-9e59-f3bb80327bfe","uuid":"d7205a2b-1bf3-450d-b1b0-66b7bcf43b38","timestamp":"2026-10-26T19:42:26.684Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8009"}
