{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-95ca987726e92d87","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8009-E002\n3\ttitle: 'Security analysis: bypass vectors for workspace-aware artifact serving'\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8009\n5\tcomponent: MLflow\n6\tteam: MLflow Team\n7\ttype: Investigation\n8\tpriority: P0\n9\tdependencies: []\n10\tinvestigation_signals:\n11\t  question_specificity: 1\n12\t  source_accessibility: 1\n13\t  local_runnability: 1\n14\t  cluster_hardware_dependence: -1\n15\t  human_judgment_required: 0\n16\timplementation_type: null\n17\tai_signals: null\n18\tai_implementability: Medium\n19\tai_implementability_score: 2\n20\tjira_key: null\n21\tbranch: null\n22\tgated_by: null\n23\tgate_failure_impact: null\n24\t---\n25\t# Security analysis: bypass vectors for workspace-aware artifact serving\n26\t\n27\t## Description\n28\t\n29\tAdversarial security investigation for a tenant-isolation enforcement change. This strategy\n30\tmakes artifact access control a security boundary in multi-tenant deployments (workspace\n31\tmembership validation with a fail-closed mandate), so a dedicated bypass-vector analysis is\n32\trequired before the main implementation. The investigation enumerates the ways an artifact\n33\trequest could evade workspace validation in the planned split-server topology and produces\n34\tthe mitigation requirements that the server, plugin, and operator implementations must\n35\tsatisfy.\n36\t\n37\t## Scope\n38\t\n39\t- Threat analysis of the artifact request path in workspace-aware artifacts-only mode:\n40\t  artifact paths that do not map to an experiment, malformed or manipulated paths (including\n41\t  encoding and traversal variants), and paths that could resolve to the wrong workspace.\n42\t- Analysis of topology-level bypass vectors in the split-server deployment: artifact requests\n43\t  reaching an artifacts-only instance that lacks workspace flags (misconfiguration), traffic\n44\t  routing that sends artifact requests to an instance without workspace validation, and any\n45\t  residual artifact-serving surface on the main tracking deployment that skips validation.\n46\t- Derivation of concrete mitigation requirements per surface: server-side request validation,\n47\t  plugin-side resolution hardening, and operator-side configuration validation (the strategy's\n48\t  risk mitigation: operator validation should reject configurations where the artifacts-only\n49\t  instance lacks workspace flags).\n50\t- If short-TTL caching of workspace membership decisions is introduced for latency, analysis\n51\t  of cache staleness/invalidation as a bypass vector (stale membership decisions must not\n52\t  grant access after a workspace assignment changes).\n53\t- Deliverable: a bypass-vector analysis document mapping each vector to a determination, the\n54\t  required mitigation, and the implementation scope (server / plugin / operator) that must\n55\t  carry it.\n56\t\n57\t## Acceptance Criteria\n58\t\n59\t- The analysis enumerates the request-path and topology-level bypass vectors listed above\n60\t  and, for each, records a determination (possible under the planned design / mitigated by\n61\t  design) with supporting evidence.\n62\t- Every vector determined possible has a corresponding mitigation requirement assigned to a\n63\t  specific implementation scope (server request handling, plugin resolution, or operator\n64\t  configuration validation).\n65\t- The operator-side validation requirements (rejecting misconfigured split-server\n66\t  deployments where the artifacts-only instance would lack workspace flags) are stated\n67\t  precisely enough to serve as acceptance criteria for the operator implementation.\n68\t- Any caching-related staleness vector has a defined invalidation/consistency requirement\n69\t  consistent with the fail-closed mandate.\n70\t\n71\t## HLR Traceability\n72\t\n73\tInvestigation epic — no HLR ownership. De-risks HLR-2 and HLR-3 (tenant isolation and\n74\tfail-closed denial) and informs the configuration-validation aspect of HLR-5 and HLR-6.\n75\t\n76\t## Risks / Notes\n77\t\n78\t- Workspace authorization reuses the existing auth plugin infrastructure; per Staff Engineer\n79\t  input, this analysis must not prescribe Kubernetes API mechanics (SelfSubjectAccessReview\n80\t  details are an mlflow-kubernetes-plugins implementation concern, not strategy-level).\n81\t- The kube-rbac-proxy sidecar and `--app-name kubernetes-auth` authentication layer are\n82\t  unchanged by this strategy and out of analysis scope except where they interact with the\n83\t  new validation path.\n84\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-871f804eb52a36b1","agent_id":"aea88d3ef849ba58d","session_id":"36784e31-2123-4326-9e59-f3bb80327bfe","uuid":"d902514e-3e51-4963-81c2-286e08d9d0da","timestamp":"2026-10-26T19:46:17.137Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-95ca987726e92d87","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-8009 decomposition"}
