eview contract, the absence of this edge is not reported as a missing\n68\tdependency. No implementation-type ordering rules (7/8/10) apply — no\n69\tkonflux-onboarding/repo-onboarding/license-validation epics exist (all components are\n70\tin-platform per the strategy's \"internal, exists\" dependency table).\n71\t\n72\tConditional branch: BRANCH-B-E009 sets both `branch: B` (matching the filename) and\n73\t`gated_by: RHAISTRAT-8009-E004`, and E004 appears in its `dependencies`. No main-plan epic\n74\tlists the branch epic in its `dependencies` (E008's conditional verification of the branch\n75\tis described in its Risks/Notes, not as a dependency — the correct shape).\n76\t\n77\tCross-check completeness: no data/artifact dependency discoverable from epic content is\n78\tmissing from the DAG. E008's consumption of the failure-mode matrix flows transitively\n79\tthrough E006 (which implements it); E002's mitigation requirements flow to E005/E006/E007\n80\tvia direct edges. Critical path E001 → E005 → E006 → E008 (length 4) is reasonable for an\n81\tM-L strategy (heuristic range 4-5).\n82\t\n83\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n84\t\n85\tAll epics carry component `MLflow` / team `MLflow Team`. The strategy assigns all three\n86\tcomponents (MLflow, MLflow Operator, mlflow-kubernetes-plugins) to the same team, and\n87\t`.context/rhai-components.txt` has no canonical entries for the operator or plugins — the\n88\tcollapse to the closest canonical name (`MLflow`) is documented in the Component Mappings\n89\ttable and flagged as Health Warning 1. This is the load-bearing correct behavior\n90\t(`submit.py` strips non-canonical names). Investigations are exempt from the component\n91\tboundary rule; the implementation epics divide along the real architectural seams (plugin /\n92\tserver / operator / cross-component validation).\n93\t\n94\tSize check — classified before flagging: E006 (~1-2 sprints), E007 (~2-3 sprints), and\n95\tE008 (~1-2 sprints) each exceed the ~2-week guideline but are one logical change kept\n96\twhole with a recorded estimate, a why-not-split rationale, and a concrete conditional\n97\tre-split trigger in Health Warnings 7-9. This is the defensible trade-off shape — recorded\n98\tas watch items, not docked and not flagged for splitting.\n99\t\n100\tScope duplication: none. E006's scenario ACs and E008's end-to-end verification of the\n101\tsame scenarios are implementation-AC versus deployed-verification of one surface, with the\n102\treasoning recorded (Health Warning 10) — not two epics building the same surface. E002 and\n103\tE003 produce distinct deliverables (bypass-vector analysis vs. failure-mode matrix) on\n104\tdistinct questions. No work-product type mixing: the only documentation work (noting\n105\tnon-standard artifact root limitations in E006) is an incidental touch-up folded into the\n106\toriginating epic, per the exception.\n107\t\n108\t### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n109\t\n110\tAll four Investigations are genuine: each outcome changes downstream scope or existence\n111\t(E001 → verify-and-extend vs. refactor; E002 → remediation requirements across three epics;\n112\tE003 → the server's error-handling ACs; E004 → bounded outcome selecting the conditional\n113\tbranch). No informational-only investigation. All Implementation epics produce concrete\n114\tartifacts (code, chart templates, test scenarios, configuration).\n115\t\n116\tGating metadata: every epic with a non-null `gated_by` has `gate_failure_impact` with both\n117\t`action` and `fallback_approach` populated. Every epic listing an Investigation in\n118\t`dependencies` has `gated_by` set (E005, E006 → E001; E007, branch → E004), and every\n119\t`gated_by` target appears in that epic's direct `dependencies` list. Since `gated_by` is\n120\tsingle-valued, each epic records its primary gate; E006's and E007's `fallback_approach`\n121\ttext also encodes the security/fail-mode `add_remediation` branches declared in the\n122\tsummary. One minor gap: E005's `fallback_approach` covers only the E001 coupling/rewrite\n123\tscenario and does not mention the security-remediation branch that the DAG Justification\n124\tdeclares for the E002→E005 edge (see issues list) — minor, does not affect scoring.\n125\t\n126\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\n127\t\n128\tType-appropriate signal sets are used throughout: all four Investigations carry\n129\t`investigation_signals` (5-signal rubric); all five Implementations including the branch\n130\tepic carry `ai_signals` (9-signal rubric). Signal files exist for every epic (9/9) and the\n131\trationale tables match the frontmatter values exactly.\n132\t\n133\tCalibration against `.context`: the strategy's architecture premises are corroborated by\n134\tthe context files — mlflow.md documents the `kubernetes-auth` plugin from\n135\t`mlflow-kubernetes-plugins` (v1.5.0), the `--enable-workspaces` /\n136\t`--workspace-store-uri kubernetes://` workspace isolation, and the Dockerfile.konflux CMD\n137\tflags; mlflow-operator.md documents the Helm-in-operator pattern with server-side apply and\n138\tconditional Gateway API HTTPRoute creation via runtime CRD discovery. The `architecture_claims:\n139\t1` scores cite these files and are calibrated. Notably honest: Health Warning 6 records\n140\tthat `--artifacts-only` itself is not corroborated by mlflow.md (verified — the CMD in\n141\tmlflow.md does not include it), and E001's scope carries it as a verification item.\n142\t\n143\tBorderline values considered and found justified: E004's `local_runnability: 0` is\n144\tcoherent with its `cluster_hardware_dependence: -1` (the gating decision is derivable from\n145\tsource, but HTTPRoute behavior cannot be exercised as a plain local binary); E005's\n146\t`repo_access: 1` is grounded in the strategy's \"internal, exists\" dependency\n147\tclassification and the architecture context's documentation of the package, with the\n148\tresidual midstream-access risk surfaced in Health Warning 2 and E001. E008's neutral\n149\tscores (pattern_precedent/adapter_pattern/existing_foundation 0) are correctly reasoned\n150\tfor a new validation suite. No computed-score arithmetic checked (pipeline-computed).\n151\t\n152\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n153\t\n154\tEvery epic has testable acceptance criteria derived from the strategy. Rule-mandated ACs:\n155\tE006 carries the rollback/feature-flag AC (behavior activates only when both flags are\n156\tset; removing either restores prior behavior); E007 carries the opt-in/rollback AC for the\n157\ttopology change; no konflux chain exists (Rule 25 N/A); no docs-authoring epic exists\n158\t(Rule 24 N/A — Documentation Support is \"Pending human review\", not \"Yes\", so Rule 11 does\n159\tnot apply). The operator epic's rendered-Deployment AC correctly gates the\n160\tconfiguration-reference coupling to the server work in lieu of a DAG edge.\n161\t\n162\tSoft-AC scan: no AC states an externally-controlled schedule or availability\n163\tprecondition. E006's latency AC is written on the epic's own deliverable (measure and\n164\treport per-request overhead; implement caching where the agreed target is exceeded), with\n165\tthe absent latency target recorded in Risks/Notes and Health Warning 5 — the measure-then-\n166\toptimize shape, not a misfiled precondition. Draft-ID scan: no epic body references\n167\tsibling epics by draft ID (grep over all bodies is clean); cross-epic work is described in\n168\tplain terms (\"the routing design investigation\", \"the failure-mode to response matrix\").\n169\t\n170\t### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n171\t\n172\tFull scope coverage: flag composition, workspace validation with 403 denial, fail-closed\n173\tbehavior, single-tenant backwards compatibility (HLR-1..4 → E006), split-server operator\n174\ttopology (HLR-5 → E007), artifact traffic routing (HLR-6 → E007 + branch), plugin\n175\tresolution (HLR-7 → E005), audit logging (HLR-8 → E006). All six strategy acceptance\n176\t