{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8dd5f59d270d5544","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8009\n3\tepic_count: 8\n4\tcritical_path_length: 4\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\trevised: true\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-8009: Workspace-aware artifact serving for multi-tenant MLflow deployments\n11\t\n12\tStrategy effort M-L (4-7 sprints), three strategy components (MLflow, MLflow Operator,\n13\tmlflow-kubernetes-plugins), one team (MLflow Team). Triage (Step 0): not below threshold\n14\t(M-L scope spanning three architecturally distinct sub-systems — server flag composition,\n15\toperator split-server topology, plugin workspace resolution — that warrant separate PR\n16\treview cycles); not documentation-only (active code changes in all three components).\n17\tProceeded to full decomposition.\n18\t\n19\t## Staff Engineer Input Overrides (Step 1.5)\n20\t\n21\tThe Staff Engineer / SME Input section is non-empty and takes precedence. Three directives,\n22\tall reflected in the epic scopes:\n23\t\n24\t1. **No new flag — combine existing flags.** No epic introduces `--artifacts-only-workspace-aware`\n25\t   or a `spec.artifactsOnly.workspaceAware` CR field. The server epic composes the existing\n26\t   `--artifacts-only` and `--enable-workspaces` flags; the operator epic propagates existing\n27\t   workspace configuration to the artifacts-only instance. (The strategy body already\n28\t   incorporates this correction; the decomposition enforces it as an acceptance criterion on\n29\t   both the plugin and server epics.)\n30\t2. **Operator work is a split deployment, not a config toggle.** The operator epic delivers a\n31\t   dedicated artifacts-only Deployment with its own Service and HTTPRoute plus artifact\n32\t   traffic routing from the main tracking deployment — a split-server topology. No epic\n33\t   treats this as a single CR-field toggle.\n34\t3. **SSAR detail is over-specified.** Epics state that workspace authorization reuses the\n35\t   existing auth plugin infrastructure without dictating Kubernetes API mechanics\n36\t   (SelfSubjectAccessReview against `mlflow.kubeflow.org` pseudo-resources is removed from\n37\t   all epic scopes). The kube-rbac-proxy sidecar and `--app-name kubernetes-auth` setup are\n38\t   unchanged.\n39\t\n40\t## Component Mappings (canonical Jira component vocabulary)\n41\t\n42\t`.context/rhai-components.txt` has no canonical entries for \"MLflow Operator\" or\n43\t\"mlflow-kubernetes-plugins\". Every epic carries the closest canonical name **MLflow**:\n44\t\n45\t| Strategy component | Canonical component | Note |\n46\t|---|---|---|\n47\t| MLflow | MLflow | exact match |\n48\t| MLflow Operator | MLflow | no canonical \"MLflow Operator\"; closest canonical name is MLflow |\n49\t| mlflow-kubernetes-plugins | MLflow | no canonical entry; plugin is part of the MLflow serving surface |\n50\t\n51\tAll epic `component` frontmatter values are `MLflow`; the per-epic spans are visible in the\n52\tEpic List below. `submit.py` would silently strip any non-canonical name, so this mapping is\n53\tload-bearing for Jira labeling.\n54\t\n55\t## Open Question and Assumption Disposition\n56\t\n57\t| Strategy unknown | Disposition |\n58\t|---|---|\n59\t| Does workspace store / plugin initialization depend on tracking store availability? (OQ, MLflow Team) | **Investigation E001** — gates the plugin and server epics (`rewrite`): the answer determines verify-and-extend vs. refactor into an independent initialization path. |\n60\t| How should the operator configure artifact traffic routing, and does the tracking server need a config change? (OQ, MLflow Team) | **Investigation E004** — gates the operator epic (`rewrite`); bounded outcomes produce conditional branch `BRANCH-B-E009` (server-side delegation config) if required. |\n61\t| Artifact request paths carry sufficient information to resolve workspace membership? (assumption, needs validation) | Folded into **E001** (feasibility under the standard S3 artifact store configuration) and **E002** (adversarial/malformed paths must be denied). |\n62\t| Helm chart rendering supports a second Deployment without structural changes? (assumption, needs validation) | Acceptance criterion on the operator epic E007 — the epic exists and proceeds identically either way (failure = fix within the epic). |\n63\t| `--artifacts-only` composable with `--enable-workspaces` without breaking either flag individually? (assumption, needs validation) | Acceptance criterion on the server epic E006 (backwards-compatibility ACs; failure = fix within the epic). The epic's deliverable is the composability itself. |\n64\t| Single-tenant deployments do not set `--enable-workspaces`? (assumption, needs validation) | Acceptance criterion on E006 and regression scenario in E008. |\n65\t| Acceptable latency overhead per artifact request? (OQ, PM/Engineering) | **Non-gating** — product decision; captured as a measure-then-optimize AC on E006 (short-TTL caching if the agreed target is exceeded, per the strategy's risk mitigation). No epic's existence or approach changes. |\n66\t| Audit telemetry channel for workspace denials? (OQ, PM/Security) | **Non-gating** — HLR-8 already commits to server logs; captured as an AC on E006. If PM/Security later selects a different channel (OTLP, K8s audit log), scope extends additively — see Health Warnings. |\n67\t\n68\t**Safety-critical handling (Rule 19):** this strategy enforces tenant-boundary authorization\n69\t(workspace-membership access control with a fail-closed mandate) — an RBAC-class security\n70\tmechanism. Rule 19 therefore generates a security Investigation (E002, bypass vectors) and a\n71\tfail-mode Investigation (E003, fail-closed confirmation), both blocking the main server\n72\timplementation; E002 also blocks the operator epic (misconfiguration bypass).\n73\t\n74\t## Epic List\n75\t\n76\t| ID | Title | Type | Component (canonical) | Team | Priority |\n77\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n78\t| E001 | Validate workspace resolution dependencies in the artifacts-only server context | Investigation | MLflow (mlflow-kubernetes-plugins + MLflow server init) | MLflow Team | P0 |\n79\t| E002 | Security analysis: bypass vectors for workspace-aware artifact serving | Investigation | MLflow | MLflow Team | P0 |\n80\t| E003 | Failure-mode analysis for fail-closed workspace-aware artifact serving | Investigation | MLflow | MLflow Team | P0 |\n81\t| E004 | Determine artifact traffic routing design for the split-server topology | Investigation | MLflow (MLflow Operator) | MLflow Team | P1 |\n82\t| E005 | Extend mlflow-kubernetes-plugins workspace resolution for the artifacts-only context | Implementation | MLflow (mlflow-kubernetes-plugins) | MLflow Team | P0 |\n83\t| E006 | Workspace-aware artifacts-only serving in the MLflow tracking server | Implementation | MLflow | MLflow Team | P0 |\n84\t| E007 | MLflow operator split-server topology for workspace-aware artifact serving | Implementation | MLflow (MLflow Operator) | MLflow Team | P1 |\n85\t| E008 | Integration validation of workspace-aware artifact serving across the split-server topology | Implementation | MLflow | MLflow Team | P1 |\n86\t| BRANCH-B-E009 | Main tracking server artifact-traffic delegation configuration (branch B) | Implementation | MLflow | MLflow Team | P1 |\n87\t\n88\tEpic count: 8 main-plan epics plus 1 conditional branch epic. Branch epics are excluded from\n89\tthe main-plan critical path.\n90\t\n91\t## Dependency DAG\n92\t\n93\t```mermaid\n94\tgraph TD\n95\t    E001[\"E001 Workspace resolution dependencies (Inv)\"] --> E005[\"E005 Plugin workspace resolution (Impl)\"]\n96\t    E001 --> E006[\"E006 Server workspace-aware artifacts-only serving (Impl)\"]\n97\t    E002[\"E002 Security bypass analysis (Inv)\"] --> E005\n98\t    E002 --> E006\n99\t    E002 --> E007[\"E007 Operator split-server topology (Impl)\"]\n100\t    E003[\"E003 Failure-mode analysis (Inv)\"] --> E006\n101\t    E004[\"E004 Routing design (Inv)\"] --> E007\n102\t    E004 --> E009B[\"BRANCH-B-E009 Server delegation config (branch)\"]\n103\t    E005 --> E006\n104\t    E005 --> E008[\"E008 Integration validation (Impl)\"]\n105\t    E006 --> E008\n106\t    E007 --> E008\n107\t```\n108\t\n109\tCritical path (main plan): E001 → E005 → E006 → E008 (length 4).\n110\t\n111\t## DAG Justification\n112\t\n113\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n114\t|---|---|---|\n115\t| E001 → E005 | Rule 3 (Investigation gate) | The plugin epic's scope (verify-and-extend vs. refactor workspace resolution into an independent init path) is determined by the init-coupling outcome. True gate: `rewrite`. |\n116\t| E001 → E006 | Rule 3 (Investigation gate) | The server's workspace-provider initialization approach depends on whether resolution can function without tracking stores. True gate: `rewrite`. |\n117\t| E002 → E005 | Rule 3 / Rule 19 | Bypass analysis may require plugin-side resolution hardening (unresolvable/ambiguous paths must be deniable). True gate: `add_remediation`. |\n118\t| E002 → E006 | Rule 19 | Security investigation blocks the main implementation (tenant-isolation enforcement). True gate: `add_remediation`. |\n119\t| E002 → E007 | Rule 3 / Rule 19 | Operator-side configuration validation requirements (reject split-server configs lacking workspace flags) derive from the bypass analysis. True gate: `add_remediation`. |\n120\t| E003 → E006 | Rule 19 | Fail-mode investigation blocks the main implementation; its failure-mode → response matrix becomes the server's error-handling ACs. True gate: `add_remediation`. |\n121\t| E004 → E007 | Rule 3 (Investigation gate) | The routing design determines the operator's routing implementation (HTTPRoute design; whether a server-side delegation config is needed). True gate: `rewrite`. |\n122\t| E004 → BRANCH-B-E009 | Rule 3 (conditional branch) | Branch B exists only if the routing investigation selects server-side delegation; gated_by E004, `remove` if outcome A holds. |\n123\t| E005 → E006 | Rule 12 (library → consumer) | The server builds against the plugin's workspace resolution init path (possibly refactored); plugin is a library consumed by the server. |\n124\t| E005 → E008, E006 → E008, E007 → E008 | Verification ordering | Integration validation exercises the deployed capabilities of all three implementations; it cannot run before they land. |\n125\t| *(deliberate non-edge)* server impl ⇸ operator impl | Rule 13 / Rule 14 | The operator's rendered Deployment references the server's flag combination — a configuration reference, not a build-against artifact (different repos, no shared artifact). The coupling is expressed as the operator epic's \"artifacts-only Deployment runs with the combined flags\" AC, never as a DAG edge; the two epics run in parallel. |\n126\t\n127\t## Conditional Branches\n128\t\n129\t**E004 (routing design) has two bounded outcomes:**\n130\t\n131\t- **Outcome A — ingress-level routing suffices** (artifact traffic routed to the dedicated\n132\t  instance at the Gateway API / HTTPRoute level; no server change): the main plan covers it\n133\t  entirely within the operator epic; no branch file.\n134\t- **Outcome B — the main tracking server needs a configuration change to delegate artifact\n135\t  operations**: additional server-side work not owned by any main-plan epic, planned as\n136\t  `RHAISTRAT-8009-BRANCH-B-E009.md` (branch=B, gated_by E004, `remove` on outcome A).\n137\t\n138\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n139\t\n140\tHLRs numbered in strategy order.\n141\t\n142\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) | Notes |\n143\t|---|---|---|---|\n144\t| HLR-1 `--artifacts-only` combinable with `--enable-workspaces` + `--workspace-store-uri` | P0 | E006 | End-to-end verified in E008. |\n145\t| HLR-2 Artifact requests validated against workspace membership; non-members denied 403 | P0 | E006 (enabled by E005) | Resolution capability from the plugin epic; end-to-end verified in E008; bypass resistance informed by E002. |\n146\t| HLR-3 Fail-closed when workspace store unreachable | P0 | E006 | Failure-mode matrix from E003; end-to-end verified in E008. |\n147\t| HLR-4 Single-tenant / no-workspace-flag deployments retain current behavior | P0 | E006 | Regression verified in E008. |\n148\t| HLR-5 Operator deploys dedicated artifacts-only Deployment + Service + HTTPRoute | P1 | E007 | Verified in E008. |\n149\t| HLR-6 Main tracking deployment routes artifact traffic to the dedicated instance | P1 | E007 (+ BRANCH-B-E009 if routing outcome B) | Routing mechanism determined by E004. |\n150\t| HLR-7 Auth plugin workspace resolution works in artifacts-only context | P1 | E005 | Approach determined by E001. |\n151\t| HLR-8 Audit logging for workspace access denials through server logs | P2 | E006 **(bundled)** | Bundled, not split: the audit log statements are emitted at the same denial sites HLR-2 implements, in the same request-handling code — a separate epic would modify the same files twice, so the P2 work is not independently schedulable. |\n152\t\n153\tFull coverage: every HLR maps to at least one epic; no orphaned requirements.\n154\t\n155\t## Health Warnings\n156\t\n157\t1. **Component vocabulary collapse** — \"MLflow Operator\" and \"mlflow-kubernetes-plugins\" have\n158\t   no canonical Jira component entries; all epics carry canonical component `MLflow` (see\n159\t   Component Mappings). Jira component-level triage will not distinguish operator/plugin work.\n160\t2. **mlflow-kubernetes-plugins has no dedicated architecture-context file** — it is documented\n161\t   indirectly by mlflow.md as the external `kubernetes-auth` plugin package (v1.5.0, installed\n162\t   from PyPI). Per the strategy's dependency table (\"internal, exists\") it is treated as an\n163\t   existing in-platform component: **no onboarding chain generated**. Flag for verification: if\n164\t   the plugin's source repo is not accessible for midstream changes, an onboarding chain\n165\t   (repo/license/konflux) would need to be added.\n166\t3. **Strategy-level process gates pending human review** — Architecture Review, ODH/RHOAI Build\n167\t   Onboarding, Licence Validation, Accelerator/Package Support, Documentation Support, UXD\n168\t   Support, Performance Team Support are all \"Pending human review\". These are strategy-level\n169\t   gates, not epic-scoped work; no epics were generated for them. The pending architecture\n170\t   review should be resolved before implementation epics merge.\n171\t4. **Audit telemetry channel (OQ, PM/Security)** — HLR-8 commits to server logs. If\n172\t   PM/Security selects OTLP or the Kubernetes audit log instead, the server epic's audit scope\n173\t   extends additively; no structural change to this decomposition.\n174\t5. **Latency target undefined (OQ, PM/Engineering)** — no latency target exists in the RFE or\n175\t   architecture context. The server epic carries a measure-then-optimize AC (short-TTL caching\n176\t   if the agreed target is exceeded). If caching is introduced, its staleness behavior must\n177\t   satisfy the security analysis (E002).\n178\t6. **`--artifacts-only` mode is not corroborated by architecture context** — mlflow.md\n179\t   corroborates `--enable-workspaces`, `--workspace-store-uri kubernetes://`,\n180\t   `--app-name kubernetes-auth`, and `--serve-artifacts` (Dockerfile.konflux CMD) but does not\n181\t   describe the `--artifacts-only` mode. Not contradicted; noted for signal-9 honesty and as a\n182\t   verification item inside E001.\n183\t7. **Epic size — E006 (server)**: ~1-2 sprints by the strategy's effort arithmetic (above the\n184\t   ~2-week guideline). Kept whole because flag composition, workspace-provider initialization,\n185\t   and per-request validation are one server initialization/request-handling change over the\n186\t   same code path — a split would build the same request surface twice. Re-split trigger: if\n187\t   the error-handling/failure-mode work emerging from the fail-mode analysis exceeds ~half a\n188\t   sprint beyond the strategy's estimate, split the failure-mode hardening into a follow-up\n189\t   epic rather than growing this one past 2 sprints.\n190\t8. **Epic size — E007 (operator)**: ~2-3 sprints by the strategy's effort arithmetic. Kept\n191\t   whole because the dedicated Deployment/Service/HTTPRoute templates and the artifact traffic\n192\t   routing configuration are one declarative topology over the same Helm-in-operator render\n193\t   path — splitting would fragment one chart surface across epics that build the same\n194\t   templates. Re-split trigger: if the routing design (once resolved) shows the traffic-routing\n195\t   configuration work is independently schedulable beyond ~1 sprint, split HLR-6 routing\n196\t   configuration from HLR-5 resource provisioning into separate epics.\n197\t9. **Epic size — E008 (integration validation)**: ~1-2 sprints by the strategy's effort\n198\t   arithmetic. Kept whole because all scenarios share one deployed split-server test topology;\n199\t   splitting would stand up the same environment twice. Re-split trigger: if scenario\n200\t   automation exceeds the strategy's estimate, split server-level scenarios from\n201\t   topology-level scenarios along that environment boundary.\n202\t10. **Priority notes** — E005 carries P0 by priority inheritance (its own HLR-7 is P1, but it\n203\t    is a build-against prerequisite of the P0 server epic, so it blocks all P0 work). E008 is\n204\t    P1: the P0 scenarios (strategy ACs 1-4) are server-level and are duplicated as acceptance\n205\t    criteria on E006, so P0 acceptance does not wait on the P1 operator rollout; E008 is\n206\t    scheduled with the P1 split-server topology it verifies.\n207\t\n208\t## Tiered Delivery\n209\t\n210\tNot applicable. All dependencies are internal and existing (workspace store, auth plugin,\n211\tGateway API, kube-rbac-proxy per the strategy's dependency table — all \"internal, exists\");\n212\tthere is no uncertain-timing external dependency to tier around.\n213\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-80108d2f7311d22b","agent_id":"a3efa4e901997f78f","session_id":"36784e31-2123-4326-9e59-f3bb80327bfe","uuid":"fc285992-b277-477b-82f2-bc4903f8ec4a","timestamp":"2026-10-26T19:55:40.228Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-8dd5f59d270d5544","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-8009"}
